examples.question_text:
A rectangle is cut along both dashed diagonals. How many pieces are made, and what shape is each piece?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5,3);
\draw[dashed] (0,0)--(5,3) (0,3)--(5,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The diagonals meet inside the rectangle. There is one triangular piece beside each of its four sides, making four triangles.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5,3);
\draw[dashed] (0,0)--(5,3) (0,3)--(5,0);
\path (2.5,0.5) node {$1$} (4.2,1.5) node {$2$} (2.5,2.5) node {$3$} (0.8,1.5) node {$4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
Cut this parallelogram along the dashed line. Describe how to move the left triangular piece to make a rectangle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(5,0)--(6,3)--(1,3)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (1,0)--(1,3);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Translate the left triangle to the right by the length of the base. Its sloping side meets the parallelogram's right edge. The pieces form a rectangle without gaps or overlaps.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (1,0) rectangle (6,3);
\draw[dashed] (5,0)--(6,3);
\path (5.65,0.8) node {$T$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$T$ marks the translated triangle. Its size and orientation have stayed the same.

examples.question_text:
Two matching triangles have the same orientation. Describe how to move the second triangle to join the first and form a parallelogram.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(4,0)--(1,2)--cycle;
\draw (6,0)--(10,0)--(7,2)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Rotate the second triangle through $180^{\circ}$, then translate it so its long sloping side meets the matching side of the first triangle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(4,0)--(5,2)--(1,2)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (4,0)--(1,2);
\path (1.6,0.65) node {$A$} (3.4,1.35) node {$B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$A$ is the first triangle and $B$ is the moved copy. The outside boundary is a parallelogram; neither triangle has changed size.

examples.question_text:
Make one straight cut in this step-shaped figure, then rearrange the two pieces to make a rectangle. Describe the cut and movements. All corners are right angles.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(7,0)--(7,1)--(4,1)--(4,3)--(0,3)--cycle;
\path (3.5,0) node[below] {$7$} (0,1.5) node[left] {$3$} (7,0.5) node[right] {$1$} (5.5,1) node[above] {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Cut vertically where the step begins. This separates a $3$-by-$1$ rectangle from a $4$-by-$3$ rectangle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0)--(7,0)--(7,1)--(4,1)--(4,3)--(0,3)--cycle;
\draw[dashed] (4,0)--(4,1);
\path (2,1.5) node {$A$} (5.5,0.5) node {$B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Rotate $B$ through $90^{\circ}$ and translate it beside $A$. Its sides now measure $1$ by $3$, so the pieces form a $5$-by-$3$ rectangle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black},draw=black]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5,3);
\draw[dashed] (4,0)--(4,3);
\path (2,1.5) node {$A$} (4.5,1.5) node {$B$} (2.5,0) node[below] {$5$} (0,1.5) node[left] {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Every piece is used once without overlap.
